" (https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20%26%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf)\nciteturn49view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 4; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20%26%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20&%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf; Total lines: 141\nL0@P0: URL: https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20&%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf\nL1@P0: On a Diffuse Reflection of the α-Particles\nL2@P0: Proc. Roy. Soc. 1909 A\nL3@P0: vol. 82, p. 495-500\nL4@P0: By H. GEIGER, Ph.D., John Harling Fellow, and E. MARSDEN, Hatfield Scholar, University of Manchester.\nL5@P0: (Communicated by Prof. E. Rutherford, F.R.S. Received May 19, -- Read June 17, 1909.)\nL6@P0: 495\nL7@P0: When β-particles fall on a plate, a strong radiation emerges from the same side of the plate as that on which the\nL8@P0: β-particles fall. This radiation is regarded by many observers as a secondary radiation, but more recent\nL9@P0: experiments seem to show that it consists mainly of primary β-particles, which have been scattered inside the\nL10@P0: material to such an extent that they emerge again at the same side of the plate.* For α-particles a similar effect\nL11@P0: has not previously been observed, and is perhaps not to be expected on account of the relatively small scattering\nL12@P0: which α-particles suffer in penetrating matter.**\nL13@P0: In the following experiments, however, conclusive evidence was found of the existence of a diffuse reflection of\nL14@P0: the α-particles. A small fraction of the α-particles falling upon a metal plate have their directions changed to\nL15@P0: such an extent that they emerge again at the side of incidence. To form an idea of the way in which this effect\nL16@P0: takes place, the following three points were investigated:\nL17@P0: (I) The relative amount of reflection from different metals.\nL18@P0: (II) The relative amount of reflection from a metal of varying thickness.\nL19@P0: (III) The fraction of the incident α-particles which are reflected.\nL20@P0: * See Schmidt, 'Jahrbuch der Radioaktivitat und Electronik,' vol. 5, p. 471, 1908.\nL21@P0: ** Rutherford, 'Phil. Mag.,' vol. 12, p. 143, 1906; H. Geiger, 'Roy. Soc. Proc.,' A, vol. 81, p. 174,1908.\nL22@P0: 496\nL23@P0: For the observation of the reflected particles the scintillation method was used in all experiments. With regard to\nL24@P0: the details of the method we refer to the papers of Regener* and of Rutherford and Geiger.**\nL25@P0: On account of the fact that the amount of reflection is very small, it was necessary to use a very intense source of\nL26@P0: α-rays. A tube was employed similar to that which has been proved to be a suitable source in the scattering\nL27@P0: experiments of one of us.*** This source consisted of a glass tube AB (fig. 1), drawn down conically and filled\nL28@P0: with radium emanation, the end B of the tube being closed airtight by\nL29@P0: means of a mica window. The thickness of the mica was equivalent to\nL30@P0: about 1 cm. of air, so that the α-particles could easily pass through it.\nL31@P0: Since it is of importance that the gas pressure inside this tube should be\nL32@P0: as low as possible, the emanation was purified according to the\nL33@P0: methods developed by Prof. Rutherford.**** The tube contained an\nL34@P0: amount of emanation equivalent to about 20 milligrammes RaBr2 at a\nL35@P0: pressure of a few centimetres. The number of α-particles expelled per\nL36@P0: second through the window was, therefore, very great, and, on account\nL37@P0: of the small pressure inside the tube, the different ranges of the α\u0002particles from the three products (i.e. emanation, RaA, and RaC) were\nL38@P0: sharply defined.\nL39@P0: The zinc sulphide screen S (fig. 1) was fixed behind the lead plate P, in\nL40@P0: such a position that no α-particles could strike it directly. When a\nL41@P0-1: reflector was placed in the position RR at about 1 cm. from the end ofthe tube, scintillations were at once observed. At the same time the screen brightened up appreciably on account\nL42@P1: of the reflected β-particles.\nL43@P1: By means of a low power microscope, the number of scintillations per minute on a definite square millimetre of\nL44@P1: the screen was counted for reflectors of different materials. Care was taken that the different reflectors were\nL45@P1: always placed in exactly the same position.\nL46@P1: It is, of course, to be expected that the number of α-particles reflected from the plate would be different in\nL47@P1: different directions, and would also depend on the angle of incidence. In our arrangement, however, no\nL48@P1: appreciable difference was found for different angles. This is due to the fact that,\nL49@P1: * 'Verh. d. D. Phys. Ges.,' vol. 10, p. 78,1908.\nL50@P1: ** 'Roy. Soc. Proc.,' A, vol. 81, p. 141, 1908.\nL51@P1: *** Geiger, 'Roy. Soc. Proc.,' A, vol. 81, p. 174, 1908.\nL52@P1: **** 'Phil. Mag.,' August, p. 300, 1908.\nL53@P1: 497\nL54@P1: owing to the necessity of having the tube very near to the reflector, the angle of incidence varied very much. An\nL55@P1: investigation of the variation of the effect with the angles of incidence and emergence would necessitate a.\nL56@P1: parallel and very intense source of homogeneous α-rays, which can, however, not easily be realised.\nL57@P1: In the following table the number of scintillations observed per minute are given in column 3; in column 4 the\nL58@P1: ratio to the atomic weight is calculated, and it can be seen that this ratio decreases with decreasing atomic\nL59@P1: weight. The case of lead appears to be an exception which may be due to slight impurities in the lead.\nL60@P1: 1.\nL61@P1: Metal\nL62@P1: 2.\nL63@P1: Atomic weight, A\nL64@P1: 3.\nL65@P1: Number of scintillations\nL66@P1: per minute, Z.\nL67@P1: 4.\nL68@P1: A/Z.\nL69@P1: Lead 207 62 30\nL70@P1: Gold 197 67 34\nL71@P1: Platinum 195 63 33\nL72@P1: Tin 119 34 28\nL73@P1: Silver 108 27 25\nL74@P1: Copper 64 14·5 23\nL75@P1: Iron 56 10·2 18·5\nL76@P1: Aluminium. 27 3·4 12·5\nL77@P1: Even in the absence of any reflector about one scintillation per minute. was observed. It was easy to show that\nL78@P1: this was due to a reflection from the air through which the α-particles passed. The numbers on the table are\nL79@P1: corrected for this effect. It is interesting to note here that for β-particles the number of reflected particles also\nL80@P1: decreases with the atomic weight of the reflector.* But while for β-particles the number reflected from gold is\nL81@P1: only about twice as, great as for aluminium, for α-particles the same ratio amounts to about twenty.\nL82@P1: (II) We have already pointed out that the diffuse reflection of the α-particles is a consequence of their scattering.\nL83@P1: According to this point of view, the number of particles reflected must vary with the thickness of the reflecting\nL84@P1: screen. Since gold can be obtained in very thin and uniform foils, different numbers of these foils were used as\nL85@P1: reflectors. Each foil was equivalent in stopping power to about 0·4 mm. of air. It was necessary to mount the foils\nL86@P1: on glass plates, but the number reflected from the glass itself was found to be very small compared even with the\nL87@P1: number from one gold foil. The curve, fig. 2, gives the result of the measurements.\nL88@P1: * McClelland, 'Dublin Trans.,' vol. 9, p. 9, 1906.\nL89@P1-2: 498The number of scintillations which were due to the reflection from the air is subtracted from each reading. The\nL90@P2: first point on the curve represents the number of scintillations observed for a glass plate alone as reflector; the\nL91@P2: last point (marked 30) gives the number of scintillations when a thick gold plate was used.\nL92@P2: The curve is similar to those which have been obtained for the reflection of the β-particles.* It brings out clearly\nL93@P2: that the reflection is not a surface but a volume effect.\nL94@P2: Compared, however, with the thickness of gold which an α-particle can penetrate, the effect is confined to a\nL95@P2: relatively thin layer. In our experiment, about half of the reflected particles were reflected from a layer equivalent\nL96@P2: to about 2 min. of air. If the high velocity and mass of the a-particle be taken into account, it seems surprising\nL97@P2: that some of the α-particles, as the experiment shows, can be tamed within a layer of 6 x 10¯5 cm. of gold\nL98@P2: through an angle of 90°, and even more. To produce a similar effect by a magnetic field, the enormous field of\nL99@P2: 109 absolute units would be required.\nL100@P2: (III) In the next experiment, an estimate of the total number of particles reflected was aimed at. For this purpose\nL101@P2: the emanation tube used in the previous experiments was unsuitable, firstly, on account of the difficulty of\nL102@P2: correctly ascertaining the number of α-particles emerging from the tube; and secondly, on account of the\nL103@P2: different ranges of the α-particles from the\nL104@P2: * McClelland, 'Phil. Mag.,' vol. 9, p. 230, 1905; 'Ann. d. Phys.,' vol. 18, p. 974, 1905; Schmidt, 'Ann. d. Phys.,' vol. 23, p. 671, 1907; 'Phys.\nL105@P2: Zeit.,' vol. 8, p. 737, 1907.\nL106@P2: 499\nL107@P2: three products: emanation, radium A, and radium C. Consequently, as\nL108@P2: radiating source, radium C, deposited on a plate of small dimensions, was\nL109@P2: used. The arrangement, which is sketched in fig. 3, was such that the α\u0002particles from the plate A fell upon the platinum reflector R, of about 1\nL110@P2: square centimetre area, at an average angle of 90°. The reflected particles\nL111@P2: were counted on different points of the screen S.\nL112@P2: No appreciable variation of the number was found with different angles\nL113@P2: of emergence, the reason of which has already been explained above.\nL114@P2: The amount of radium C deposited on the plate was determined by its γ\u0002ray activity. Assuming that 3·4 x 1010\nL115@P2:  particles are expelled per second\nL116@P2-3: from an amount of RaC equivalent to 1 gramme Ra,* the number of α-particles expelled per second from the active plate was determined. The number falling on the platinum reflector\nL117@P3: was then easily calculated from its known distance and area. To find the whole number of reflected particles, it\nL118@P3: was assumed that they were distributed uniformly round a half sphere with the middle of the reflector as centre.\nL119@P3: Three different determinations showed that of the incident α-particles about 1 in 8000 was reflected, under the\nL120@P3: described conditions.\nL121@P3: A special experiment conducted at low pressure showed that in the case of grazing incidence the number of\nL122@P3: particles reflected at a very small angle to the reflector is largely in excess of the number calculated from the\nL123@P3: above ratio. This tangential scattering is of considerable importance in some experiments; for instance, if α\u0002particles from a radio-active source are fired along a glass tube of appreciable length the conditions are very\nL124@P3: favourable for this effect. The number of scintillations counted on a screen sealed to the other end of the tube is\nL125@P3: made up not only of the particles striking the screen directly, but also of those which have been reflected from\nL126@P3: the glass walls of the tube.\nL127@P3: The correction for the latter effect may be appreciable, and would be still greater in the case of a metal tube. In\nL128@P3: the counting experiments of Rutherford and Geiger this effect did not influence the final result, the arrangement\nL129@P3: being such that the reflected particles were prevented from entering the opening of the ionisation vessel by the\nL130@P3: narrow constriction of a stopcock\nL131@P3: It appears probable that the number of reflected particles depends also upon the velocity of the α-particles falling\nL132@P3: on the reflector. In our case\nL133@P3: * Rutherford and Geiger, 'Roy. Soc. Proc.,' A, vol. 81, p. 162, 1908.\nL134@P3: 500\nL135@P3: the particles from the radium C had to travel through a little over a centimetre of air before reaching the reflector.\nL136@P3: The reflected particles had still an appreciable velocity, since, by interposing an aluminium foil of thickness\nL137@P3: equivalent in stopping power to 1/2 cm. of air, the number of scintillations counted was not changed. This might\nL138@P3: be expected from Experiment (II), which showed that the α-particles are reflected from a relatively thin surface\nL139@P3: layer of the reflector.\nL140@P3: We are indebted to Prof. Rutherford for his kind interest and advice throughout this research.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn49view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://royalsocietypublishing.org/rspa/article-pdf/82/557/495/29978/rspa.1909.0054.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://royalsocietypublishing.org/rspa/article-pdf/82/557/495/29978/rspa.1909.0054.pdf is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn49view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlehtml/1902/ct/ct9028100837\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlehtml/1902/ct/ct9028100837: (403) Forbidden"
